// BD settings for the starter app.
//
// Xcode reads these into Info.plist, which is where an app bundle keeps its
// configuration — there is no .env in an .ipa. Point your target's
// Configuration at this file (Project ▸ Info ▸ Configurations), then add the
// matching keys to Info.plist as $(BD_...) references:
//
//   BDHost             $(BD_HOST)
//   BDPublishableKey   $(BD_PUBLISHABLE_KEY)
//   BDSiteID           $(BD_SITE_ID)
//   BDAuthCallbackURL  $(BD_AUTH_CALLBACK_URL)
//
// Keep real values out of source control — copy this to
// BdStarter.local.xcconfig, add that to .gitignore, and `#include` it.

BD_HOST = https:/$()/www.biab.app

// ⚠️ PUBLISHABLE TOKEN ONLY (`pk_…`).
//
// An app ships its credential inside the binary, where `strings` on an .ipa
// finds it in seconds. BdClient traps at init if you hand it an `sk_…` key.
//
// This is less limiting than it sounds: the publishable scope set covers the
// entire customer-facing surface — storefront, cart, checkout, blog, marketing
// content, chat, forms, the whole customer portal, sign-in, and public
// custom-object reads. Only operator/admin writes need a secret key, and a
// customer app never makes those.
BD_PUBLISHABLE_KEY =

BD_SITE_ID =

// Must ALSO be registered twice or auth/start refuses:
//   1. CFBundleURLTypes in Info.plist (so the OS routes it back to the app)
//   2. the redirect URIs on your BD site
BD_AUTH_CALLBACK_URL = bdstarter:/$()/auth/callback
